.TH GGE\-TYPES 7 "generalGameEngine" "Engine Manual" "Engine Manual"
.SH NAME
gge\-types \- Color, Rect, Point, Texture, Flip, Vec2 and the constants
.SH DESCRIPTION
types.h names the handful of platform types a game touches. Each is the SDL type underneath (same memory layout, zero conversion), so the engine hands them straight to SDL, but game code never writes "SDL_". mathx.h adds \fBVec2\fR, the engine's vector type. This page is the reference for all of them and for the helpers that build them.
.PP
.SH COLOR
\fBColor\fR is {uint8_t r, g, b, a}, 0..255 each. Alpha 255 is opaque, 0 invisible, and every draw call blends, so anything in between is translucent.
.TP
\fBrgb(r, g, b)\fR
opaque color.
.TP
\fBrgba(r, g, b, a)\fR
color with alpha.
.TP
\fBCOLOR_WHITE, COLOR_BLACK, COLOR_RED, COLOR_GREEN, COLOR_BLUE, COLOR_YELLOW, COLOR_GREY, COLOR_CLEAR\fR
ready\-made constants (COLOR_CLEAR is fully transparent). They are compound\-literal macros, usable anywhere a Color is.
Conventions the modules rely on: a Color with alpha 0 means "unset" in EngineConfig.clear_color and in Menu.color / color_selected, which then use their defaults. Tints for sprite_draw_tinted(3) multiply: white leaves the sprite unchanged, \fBrgba(255, 255, 255, 128)\fR makes it half transparent, \fBrgb(255, 80, 80)\fR reddens it.
.PP
Define your palette once, in a header, as macros or \fBstatic const Color\fR values, and use only those names; a game with \fBrgb(213, 17, 98)\fR scattered through its scenes cannot be recolored.
.PP
.SH RECT
\fBRect\fR is {float x, y, w, h}: top\-left corner plus size, y growing downward. Used for draw destinations, texture source regions, hitboxes and camera views.
.TP
\fBrect(x, y, w, h)\fR
build from corner and size.
.TP
\fBrect_at(pos, w, h)\fR
corner at a Vec2 (an entity's hitbox when pos is its top\-left).
.TP
\fBrect_centered(center, w, h)\fR
centered on a Vec2.
.TP
\fBrect_center(r)\fR
the middle as a Vec2.
.TP
\fBrect_overlaps(a, b), rect_contains(r, p), rect_hit_side(a, b)\fR
the tests, in gge\-math(7).
Widths and heights are floats but sprites are whole pixels; keep hitboxes at integer sizes and let positions be fractional.
.PP
.SH POINT
\fBPoint\fR is {float x, y}. It exists only because a few SDL calls want it; game code uses Vec2 for positions. The two have the same layout.
.PP
.SH VEC2
\fBVec2\fR is {float x, y} from mathx.h: positions, velocities, directions, sizes. Built with v2(x, y); all arithmetic is by value (v2_add, v2_sub, v2_scale, v2_norm, v2_lerp, v2_len, v2_dist, v2_dot; gge\-math(7)). It is pointer\-free, so it lives inside entities in GameState.
.PP
.SH TEXTURE
\fBTexture\fR is an opaque GPU image. Games hold only pointers obtained from assets_texture(3) and pass them to draw_texture(3), sprite_from(3) and tilemap_load_csv(3). The asset cache owns every Texture; never free one. A NULL Texture is accepted by every draw call and draws nothing.
.PP
.SH FLIP
\fBFlip\fR selects mirroring for draw_texture_ex(3) and the sprite_draw family: FLIP_NONE, FLIP_H (mirror left\-right), FLIP_V (top\-bottom); combine with \fB|\fR. A right\-facing sprite drawn with FLIP_H faces left, which halves the art needed for any character.
.PP
.SH INTEGERS AND FLOATS
Positions, velocities, sizes and times are floats. Counts, indices, ids, tile coordinates, hit points and scores are ints. Convert explicitly at the boundary: \fB(int)floorf(pos.x / tile)\fR for the tile under a position (floorf, not a cast, so negative positions round toward negative infinity), \fB(float)e\->logical_w\fR when mixing with floats to keep \-Wconversion quiet if you enable it.
.PP
.SH FIXED\-WIDTH INTEGERS
Tile ids are \fBuint8_t\fR (0..255). The RNG state is \fBuint64_t\fR. Save versions are \fBuint32_t\fR. Everything else in GameState can be plain \fBint\fR, \fBfloat\fR and \fBbool\fR; the save file stores them as this machine lays them out, which is fine for a game that reads its own saves.
.PP
.SH STRINGS
There is no string type. Labels and dialog lines are \fBconst char *\fR to literals or to \fBstatic char\fR buffers; the menu and dialog modules keep the pointer, so the text must outlive them. Text that belongs in GameState (a player name) is a fixed \fBchar name[16]\fR array, which is pointer\-free and saves with the rest.
.PP
